# 3. ARCHITECTURE FIRST

Create or update:

```text
ARCHITECTURE.md
docs/AGENT-RUNTIME.md
docs/UPGRADE-06.md
```

Before extensive implementation, define the target runtime.

Recommended logical architecture:

```text
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                 ZCoder API/CLI                │
├───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│              Job Orchestrator                 │
│                                               │
│  Scheduler                                    │
│  Planner                                      │
│  Approval Engine                              │
│  Policy Engine                                │
│  Budget Manager                               │
│  Event Bus                                    │
│  Artifact Manager                             │
├───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│               Runtime Adapter                 │
│                                               │
│   DirectRuntime                               │
│   AgentSDKRuntime                             │
│   ManagedAgentRuntime                         │
├───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│              Execution Layer                  │
│                                               │
│ Workspace │ Sandbox │ Tools │ MCP │ Git       │
├───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│              Persistence Layer                │
│                                               │
│ Jobs │ Runs │ Events │ Approvals │ Artifacts  │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Names may differ.

Architecture matters more than exact filenames.

---

# 4. INTRODUCE A DURABLE JOB MODEL

Create a first-class ZCoder job abstraction.

Conceptually:

```python
Job:
    id
    task
    status
    runtime
    workspace
    created_at
    updated_at
    started_at
    completed_at

    model
    strategy
    budget

    parent_job_id
    attempt
    metadata
```

Job status must be explicit.

Suggested state machine:

```text
CREATED
   │
   ▼
PLANNING
   │
   ▼
READY
   │
   ▼
RUNNING
   │
   ├──────► WAITING_APPROVAL
   │             │
   │             └────► RUNNING
   │
   ├──────► PAUSED
   │
   ├──────► RETRYING
   │
   ├──────► BUDGET_REACHED
   │
   ├──────► CANCELLED
   │
   ├──────► FAILED
   │
   └──────► SUCCEEDED
```

Do not use arbitrary strings throughout the codebase.

Validate legal state transitions.

---

# 5. JOB IDs MUST BE DURABLE

A command such as:

```bash
zcoder run "Fix authentication tests"
```

must return a durable ZCoder job ID:

```text
job_...
```

The ID must remain usable after process restart.

Support:

```bash
zcoder jobs list
zcoder jobs show JOB_ID
zcoder jobs events JOB_ID
zcoder jobs cancel JOB_ID
zcoder jobs retry JOB_ID
zcoder jobs resume JOB_ID
```

Do not make job state purely in-memory.

---

# 6. PERSISTENCE

Introduce a clean storage abstraction.

Example:

```python
class JobStore:
    create_job(...)
    get_job(...)
    update_job(...)
    list_jobs(...)
    append_event(...)
    list_events(...)
```

Implement a lightweight durable default suitable for local ZCoder use.

SQLite is acceptable if it fits current architecture.

Do not introduce PostgreSQL merely for architectural prestige.

However, the interface should not make future PostgreSQL support impossible.

Use migrations/versioning for persistent schema.

---

# 7. EVENT STORE

Every meaningful runtime transition should generate a structured event.

Example:

```text
job.created
job.planning_started
job.plan_created
job.started

agent.started
agent.message
agent.tool_requested
agent.tool_approved
agent.tool_denied
agent.tool_started
agent.tool_completed

workspace.changed

validation.started
validation.completed

budget.warning
budget.reached

job.failed
job.succeeded
job.cancelled
```

Events need:

```python
id
job_id
sequence
type
timestamp
payload
```

Sequence ordering must be deterministic per job.

Never depend only on wall-clock ordering.

---

# 8. EVENT NORMALIZATION

Do not expose Anthropic-specific Managed Agents event shapes as the ZCoder internal domain model.

Introduce normalization:

```text
Anthropic Managed Agent Event
          │
          ▼
ManagedAgentRuntime adapter
          │
          ▼
ZCoderEvent
```

Likewise:

```text
Agent SDK callback/hook
          │
          ▼
AgentSDKRuntime
          │
          ▼
ZCoderEvent
```

Preserve provider-specific raw metadata separately where useful.

---

# 9. RUNTIME INTERFACE

Design a stable internal interface.

Conceptually:

```python
class AgentRuntime(Protocol):

    async def start(...):
        ...

    async def resume(...):
        ...

    async def cancel(...):
        ...

    async def send(...):
        ...

    async def events(...):
        ...

    async def status(...):
        ...
```

Do not force runtimes to fake unsupported semantics.

Expose capabilities:

```python
RuntimeCapabilities(
    durable_sessions=...,
    resume=...,
    fork=...,
    multiagent=...,
    sandbox=...,
    memory=...,
    webhooks=...,
    deployments=...,
)
```

---

# 10. DIRECT RUNTIME

Preserve the current custom Messages API/tool-loop architecture.

Wrap it as:

```text
DirectRuntime
```

Do NOT rewrite known-good behavior merely to satisfy the new abstraction.

It remains useful for:

* low latency
* precise request control
* custom tool loops
* specialized workflows
* compatibility testing

---

# 11. AGENT SDK RUNTIME

Implement or consolidate:

```text
AgentSDKRuntime
```

Use current official Claude Agent SDK behavior.

Support where available:

* built-in filesystem tools
* command execution
* web/search tools
* hooks
* subagents
* permissions
* sessions
* resume
* fork
* MCP
* skills
* commands
* project memory
* plugins

Do not recreate the Agent SDK's agent loop unnecessarily.

ZCoder's role is orchestration, policy, persistence and observability around it.

---

# 12. MANAGED AGENT RUNTIME

Implement:

```text
ManagedAgentRuntime
```

as a true adapter over Anthropic Managed Agents.

Do not treat it as Agent SDK over HTTP.

Map correctly:

```text
ZCoder job
    │
    └── Managed Agent session

ZCoder runtime event
    │
    └── Managed Agent event/thread event

ZCoder runtime config
    │
    ├── agent
    └── environment
```

Persist remote IDs:

```text
managed_agent_id
managed_environment_id
managed_session_id
managed_thread_id
deployment_id
```

Never use those IDs as ZCoder's primary domain identity.

---

# 13. EXECUTION BACKEND SELECTION

Support explicit:

```bash
zcoder run TASK --runtime direct
zcoder run TASK --runtime agent-sdk
zcoder run TASK --runtime managed
```

Also support:

```bash
--runtime auto
```

but AUTO must be explainable.

Example decision factors:

```text
needs durable cloud execution
needs local uncommitted files
needs self-hosted sandbox
needs long-running task
needs interactive approval
requires private-network MCP
cost budget
runtime availability
```

Provide:

```bash
zcoder runtime explain TASK
```

or equivalent debug output.

---

# 14. WORKSPACE MODEL

Create first-class workspace handling.

A coding job should know:

```text
repository root
base branch
working branch
worktree
commit base
dirty state
generated files
artifacts
```

Never let multiple autonomous jobs unknowingly edit the same worktree concurrently.

---

# 15. GIT WORKTREE ISOLATION

For concurrent local jobs, prefer isolated git worktrees where possible.

Concept:

```text
repo/
worktrees/
    job_abc/
    job_def/
```

Each job should have:

```text
base_sha
branch_name
worktree_path
```

Do not automatically delete a failed job's workspace if doing so could destroy useful changes.

---

# 16. DIRTY REPOSITORY SAFETY

Before agent mutation:

detect:

```bash
git status --porcelain
```

Policy options:

```text
deny
allow
snapshot
isolated-worktree
```

Default must avoid destroying local user changes.

Never automatically reset:

```bash
git reset --hard
```

against user-owned uncommitted work.

---

# 17. TASK PLANNING

Introduce explicit plan representation.

Example:

```python
Plan:
    objective
    assumptions
    steps
    validation
    risk
```

Optional:

```python
PlanStep:
    id
    description
    dependencies
    status
    assigned_agent
```

Do not require verbose planning for trivial tasks.

Support:

```text
planning_mode:
    none
    lightweight
    structured
```

---

# 18. DAG EXECUTION

For appropriate multi-step tasks, represent dependencies explicitly.

Example:

```text
          ┌─► backend tests ─┐
audit ────┤                 ├─► integration
          └─► frontend fix ─┘
```

Do not parallelize steps that mutate overlapping files unless conflict handling exists.

---

# 19. MULTIAGENT ORCHESTRATION

Support a first-class ZCoder multiagent strategy.

Roles may include:

```text
coordinator
architect
researcher
implementer
reviewer
tester
security-reviewer
```

Do not hardcode these as mandatory.

Allow strategy templates.

For Managed Agents, use Anthropic's actual current multiagent semantics rather than simulating them with independent sessions when unnecessary.

For Agent SDK runtime, use its legitimate subagent mechanisms where appropriate.

---

# 20. THREAD NORMALIZATION

Managed multiagent execution may expose multiple runtime threads.

Represent:

```python
AgentThread:
    id
    runtime_thread_id
    role
    parent_thread_id
    model
    status
```

ZCoder must be able to display:

```bash
zcoder jobs threads JOB_ID
```

and inspect a specific thread.

---

# 21. LIVE EVENT STREAMING

Support:

```bash
zcoder jobs watch JOB_ID
```

Output should update from structured events.

Do not rely on terminal scraping.

For Managed Agents:

correctly handle session/thread streaming and reconnect semantics according to current Anthropic documentation.

On reconnect:

* reconcile persisted event history
* avoid duplicate display
* avoid duplicate cost accounting
* continue from durable state where supported

---

# 22. EVENT IDEMPOTENCY

Events may be received more than once.

Implement deduplication using stable event IDs or provider sequence metadata where available.

Never execute a tool twice merely because an event stream reconnects.

---

# 23. OPERATOR APPROVAL ENGINE

Introduce a generic approval domain.

Example:

```python
ApprovalRequest:
    id
    job_id
    type
    requested_action
    risk
    requested_at
    status
```

Approval state:

```text
PENDING
APPROVED
DENIED
EXPIRED
CANCELLED
```

Support:

```bash
zcoder approvals list
zcoder approvals show ID
zcoder approvals approve ID
zcoder approvals deny ID
```

---

# 24. TOOL POLICY ENGINE

Tools need explicit policy.

Example categories:

```text
READ_ONLY
WORKSPACE_WRITE
PROCESS_EXECUTION
NETWORK
GIT_MUTATION
EXTERNAL_MUTATION
SECRET_ACCESS
DESTRUCTIVE
```

Policy result:

```text
ALLOW
DENY
REQUIRE_APPROVAL
```

Do not treat every shell command as equally dangerous.

---

# 25. COMMAND RISK CLASSIFICATION

At minimum identify dangerous classes such as:

```text
filesystem deletion
git force operations
privileged commands
package publishing
production deployment
credential mutation
remote data deletion
database destructive operations
```

Do not rely only on naive substring matching.

Use policy plus structured tool metadata where possible.

---

# 26. SANDBOX ABSTRACTION

Create:

```text
Sandbox
```

separate from runtime.

Possible implementations:

```text
LocalProcessSandbox
ContainerSandbox
ManagedAgentSandbox
SelfHostedManagedSandbox
```

Do not claim equivalent isolation levels.

Expose sandbox capability metadata.

---

# 27. RESOURCE LIMITS

Where the selected sandbox supports it, enforce:

```text
CPU
memory
process count
wall-clock
disk
network
output size
```

Gracefully report unsupported controls.

---

# 28. TOOL OUTPUT CONTROL

Large tool outputs must not blow up context or event storage.

Create a standard artifact/spill abstraction:

```text
small result -> inline
large result -> artifact/file + summary
```

Store:

```text
artifact ID
mime type
size
path/location
hash
```

Do not silently truncate without telling the agent/operator.

---

# 29. ARTIFACT MANAGER

First-class artifacts:

```text
patch
diff
log
test report
coverage
generated file
build artifact
review report
SBOM
```

Support:

```bash
zcoder jobs artifacts JOB_ID
zcoder artifacts show ARTIFACT_ID
```

---

# 30. FILE CHANGE TRACKING

Capture changes per job.

At minimum:

```text
files created
files modified
files deleted
diff summary
```

Before final success, calculate actual Git diff rather than trusting agent claims.

---

# 31. VALIDATION PIPELINE

A coding job must support validators.

Example:

```text
lint
typecheck
unit tests
integration tests
build
security checks
custom command
```

Represent each validator result independently.

A job cannot report SUCCEEDED merely because the agent says "done".

---

# 32. DEFINITION OF JOB SUCCESS

Success requires policy.

Example:

```python
SuccessPolicy(
    agent_completed=True,
    required_validators_passed=True,
    unresolved_approvals=False,
)
```

Expose:

```text
SUCCEEDED
SUCCEEDED_WITH_WARNINGS
FAILED_VALIDATION
```

where appropriate.

---

# 33. RETRY MODEL

Separate:

```text
transport retry
agent turn retry
job retry
validation retry
```

Do not conflate them.

Job retries should have:

```text
attempt number
previous attempt
reason
starting state
```

Preserve history.

---

# 34. CANCELLATION

Cancellation must propagate.

Example:

```text
operator
   │
zcoder job
   │
runtime
   │
agent/session
   │
tools/processes
```

Do not leave child processes or remote sessions silently running.

Cancellation must be idempotent.

---

# 35. RESUME

Support process restart recovery.

Example:

```bash
zcoder jobs resume job_123
```

Depending on runtime:

```text
DIRECT       -> local persisted context/reconstruction
AGENT SDK    -> resume SDK session where supported
MANAGED      -> reconnect to remote session
```

Do not fake resume if backend semantics cannot guarantee it.

---

# 36. FORK

When supported, expose:

```bash
zcoder jobs fork JOB_ID
```

Fork should preserve lineage:

```text
parent_job_id
parent_session_id
base_event
```

Use native backend functionality where appropriate.

---

# 37. COST MODEL

Create unified job cost accounting.

Track at minimum:

```text
input tokens
output tokens
cache reads
cache writes
thinking tokens where observable
web searches
runtime cost
tool/container costs where relevant
estimated total
actual known total
```

Differentiate:

```text
ACTUAL
PROVIDER_REPORTED
ESTIMATED
UNKNOWN
```

Never mix estimated and provider-reported values without metadata.

---

# 38. JOB BUDGET

Expose ZCoder budget policy independent from backend-specific budgets.

Example:

```bash
zcoder run TASK --budget-usd 5
```

Internally:

```text
ZCoderBudget
│
├── local advisory/enforcement logic
└── provider-native hard budget
    when genuinely supported
```

Never claim local enforcement has the same guarantees as a provider-side hard cap.

---

# 39. MANAGED AGENTS HARD BUDGET

When runtime is Managed Agents and current Anthropic API supports provider-native session budget:

use it.

Handle:

```text
budget_reached
```

as a distinct state.

Support operator action:

```bash
zcoder jobs budget JOB_ID --set 10
zcoder jobs resume JOB_ID
```

Verify exact current API semantics first.

---

# 40. MULTIAGENT BUDGET ACCOUNTING

For Managed Agents multiagent sessions, accurately account for shared session budget semantics and per-thread model usage.

Do not invent per-thread hard caps unless current Anthropic API supports them.

---

# 41. MEMORY LAYER

Define a generic ZCoder memory abstraction.

Distinguish:

```text
job-local scratch
project memory
user memory
runtime session context
Managed Agents memory store
Claude Code / Agent SDK project memory
```

Do not collapse these into one dictionary.

---

# 42. MEMORY TRUST BOUNDARY

Persistent writable memory is a security boundary.

Classify memory source:

```text
TRUSTED
UNTRUSTED
GENERATED
EXTERNAL
```

Be especially careful when agent consumes:

* web content
* issue comments
* third-party MCP output
* user uploads

and can subsequently write persistent memory.

---

# 43. MEMORY STORE SUPPORT

For Managed Agents, support the current official memory-store API where applicable.

Correctly handle:

```text
create
list
read
write
version history
attach to session
read-only
read-write
```

Respect current beta-header behavior exactly.

Do not blindly combine incompatible beta headers.

---

# 44. MEMORY VERSIONING

Expose memory history where backend supports it.

A user should be able to understand what an agent persisted.

Never make persistent memory writes invisible.

---

# 45. DREAMS

Dreams or analogous memory consolidation features must remain optional.

If current official Anthropic status is research preview or gated:

classify it accurately.

Do not make release readiness depend on preview access.

Provide an adapter only if the repository has entitlement or a mockable public contract.

---

# 46. SKILLS

Unify skill discovery without flattening different skill systems.

Model:

```text
SkillSource:
    local
    project
    platform
    managed_repository
    plugin
```

Resolve conflicts deterministically.

Expose:

```bash
zcoder skills list
zcoder skills inspect NAME
```

---

# 47. MCP REGISTRY

Represent configured MCP servers centrally.

Include:

```text
name
transport
scope
auth mode
trust level
runtime compatibility
enabled tools
```

Avoid duplicating MCP configuration separately across runtime modules.

---

# 48. MCP SECURITY

Treat remote MCP output as untrusted unless explicitly trusted.

Protect against:

```text
prompt injection
unexpected tool expansion
credential leakage
mutation without approval
oversized responses
```

Tool policy must apply to MCP tools.

---

# 49. PRIVATE NETWORK MCP

If current Managed Agents supports private-network connectivity/tunnels:

audit and integrate it through the runtime capability system.

Do not make research-preview functionality a mandatory dependency.

---

# 50. VAULTS / CREDENTIALS

Represent credential references, not credential plaintext.

Runtime events/logging must never contain secret values.

Architecture:

```text
CredentialRef
    provider
    name
    scope
    runtime
```

Do not put tokens into:

```text
job payloads
event store
logs
artifact metadata
CLI history where avoidable
```

---

# 51. WEBHOOKS

Add a generic inbound runtime-event adapter if Managed Agents webhooks are applicable.

Validate signatures/authentication using official behavior.

Webhook delivery must be:

```text
idempotent
authenticated
replay-safe where possible
```

Never trust webhook JSON merely because it reaches the endpoint.

---

# 52. WEBHOOK + STREAM RECONCILIATION

ZCoder may receive the same lifecycle fact through:

```text
SSE stream
webhook
poll
```

Normalize and deduplicate.

Do not count cost or execute side effects multiple times.

---

# 53. OUTCOMES

If current Managed Agents Outcomes API is public and applicable, support it as runtime evaluation metadata.

Do not replace ZCoder's own validation pipeline with provider outcomes.

Think:

```text
Provider Outcome
+
ZCoder Validation
=
Final Job Assessment
```

---

# 54. DEPLOYMENTS

Audit current Managed Agents deployment APIs.

If suitable for ZCoder:

support reusable agent deployment configuration.

Example:

```bash
zcoder deployments list
zcoder deployments show ID
zcoder deployments run ID
```

But avoid duplicating `ant` CLI merely for feature-count parity.

Only expose workflows relevant to ZCoder.

---

# 55. ENVIRONMENTS

Represent execution environment separately from agent configuration.

Concept:

```python
Environment:
    backend
    sandbox
    network
    resources
    secrets
    repository
```

Managed hosted and self-hosted environments must remain distinguishable.

---

# 56. SELF-HOSTED MANAGED SANDBOX

If current public Anthropic functionality supports self-hosted Managed Agent environments:

audit it.

Integrate only using documented APIs.

This could become an important ZCoder enterprise backend.

Do not invent orchestration for unsupported infrastructure.

---

# 57. AGENT PROFILE

Create reusable agent profiles.

Example:

```yaml
name: production-coder
runtime: auto
model: auto

tools:
  filesystem: true
  shell: approval
  web: true

validation:
  - pytest
  - ruff

budget:
  usd: 10
```

Support project-level config.

---

# 58. PROJECT CONFIG

Prefer a clear project configuration such as:

```text
.zcoder/
    config.yaml
    agents/
    policies/
```

Do not interfere with:

```text
.claude/
```

Claude Code and Agent SDK should remain able to consume their native configuration.

---

# 59. POLICY CONFIG

Example:

```yaml
tools:
  read:
    policy: allow

  write:
    policy: allow
    scope: workspace

  shell:
    policy: approval

  git_push:
    policy: approval

  deploy:
    policy: deny
```

Validate schema.

Fail closed on invalid security policy.

---

# 60. NETWORK POLICY

Classify network use.

Examples:

```text
deny
read-only-web
allowlisted
unrestricted
```

Do not silently permit unrestricted outbound connectivity in high-security mode.

---

# 61. APPROVAL UX

CLI interactive approval should show:

```text
Job
Agent
Requested action
Tool
Arguments
Risk
Working directory
Reason
```

Then:

```text
Approve once
Approve for job
Deny
Cancel job
```

Do not implement broad permanent "always allow everything".

---

# 62. NON-INTERACTIVE MODE

For automation:

```bash
zcoder run TASK --non-interactive
```

must have deterministic approval policy.

Never hang indefinitely waiting for an interactive prompt.

Example behavior:

```text
require-approval action encountered
→ fail/pause job
→ machine-readable state
```

---

# 63. JSON OUTPUT

All operator APIs important to automation should support JSON.

Example:

```bash
zcoder jobs show JOB --json
zcoder jobs events JOB --json
zcoder approvals list --json
```

Schemas should be documented.

---

# 64. EXIT CODES

Define stable exit codes.

At minimum distinguish:

```text
success
validation failure
approval required
budget reached
cancelled
runtime unavailable
configuration invalid
authentication
provider failure
```

Do not force automation to parse human text.

---

# 65. JOB WATCH UI

Default CLI watch view should display compact information:

```text
JOB job_123
RUNNING · managed · claude-opus-5

[03:12] planner      plan created
[03:14] implementer  editing auth.py
[03:20] tool         pytest tests/auth
[03:27] validator    42 passed

Tokens     ...
Runtime    ...
Cost       ...
Budget     ...
```

Keep raw debug mode available separately.

---

# 66. TUI

Extend TUI only after core services work.

The TUI should consume JobStore/EventStore interfaces rather than directly invoking Anthropic clients.

Views:

```text
Jobs
Job details
Threads
Events
Approvals
Artifacts
Cost
```

Do not place business logic in TUI widgets.

---

# 67. WEB CONSOLE

Same principle:

```text
Web UI
  ↓
Job Service
  ↓
Runtime
```

Not:

```text
Web UI
  ↓
Anthropic API directly
```

Add live event streaming through the shared event layer.

---

# 68. API SERVER

If Web API already exists, expose coherent endpoints.

Example:

```text
POST   /api/jobs
GET    /api/jobs
GET    /api/jobs/{id}

POST   /api/jobs/{id}/cancel
POST   /api/jobs/{id}/resume
POST   /api/jobs/{id}/retry

GET    /api/jobs/{id}/events
GET    /api/jobs/{id}/artifacts

GET    /api/approvals
POST   /api/approvals/{id}/approve
POST   /api/approvals/{id}/deny
```

Use project conventions.

---

# 69. AUTHORIZATION BOUNDARY

If the Web interface can mutate jobs:

do not assume authentication equals authorization.

Prepare architecture for:

```text
job owner
operator
administrator
```

Even if current local edition has only one user.

---

# 70. OBSERVABILITY

Instrument:

```text
job duration
planning duration
tool execution duration
validation duration
runtime errors
retries
approvals
budget exhaustion
tokens
cost
agent/thread count
```

Avoid high-cardinality metrics.

Use tracing/log context for:

```text
job_id
session_id
thread_id
```

---

# 71. STRUCTURED LOGGING

Logs should contain structured metadata:

```json
{
  "job_id": "...",
  "runtime": "managed",
  "event": "tool.completed",
  "duration_ms": 123
}
```

Never log:

```text
API keys
vault values
OAuth tokens
private key material
raw authorization headers
```

---

# 72. AUDIT TRAIL

Mutating operator actions need durable audit events:

```text
approval granted
approval denied
budget changed
job cancelled
retry requested
policy overridden
```

Record:

```text
actor
action
timestamp
target
```

For local single-user installations, actor may initially be:

```text
local-operator
```

Keep schema extensible.

---

# 73. CRASH RECOVERY

Simulate process termination during:

```text
RUNNING
WAITING_APPROVAL
VALIDATION
```

Restart ZCoder.

Ensure jobs can be classified correctly.

Do not leave ambiguous:

```text
RUNNING forever
```

Introduce recovery/reconciliation.

---

# 74. REMOTE RECONCILIATION

For Managed Agents:

local state may be stale.

Implement reconciliation:

```text
local job state
        +
remote session state
        ↓
reconciled ZCoder state
```

Do not overwrite useful local terminal states incorrectly.

---

# 75. CONCURRENCY

Support multiple jobs safely.

Define:

```text
max jobs
max local agents
max validation processes
```

Use bounded concurrency.

Do not spawn unlimited subagents/processes.

---

# 76. RATE LIMITING

Integrate existing Anthropic rate-limit knowledge.

Avoid creating a retry storm when many jobs hit 429 simultaneously.

Use shared runtime-level throttling where appropriate.

---

# 77. SCHEDULING

Implement basic scheduler abstraction.

Possible priorities:

```text
LOW
NORMAL
HIGH
```

Fairness matters.

Do not starve old jobs indefinitely.

Advanced distributed scheduling is NOT required yet.

---

# 78. JOB QUEUE

If useful, separate:

```text
queued
running
```

The persistent store is the source of truth.

Avoid building Redis/Kafka infrastructure unless repository requirements justify it.

---

# 79. IDEMPOTENT JOB SUBMISSION

Allow optional idempotency key.

Example:

```bash
zcoder run TASK --idempotency-key CI-12345
```

Duplicate submission should not accidentally launch multiple expensive jobs.

---

# 80. CHECKPOINTS

Record logical checkpoints:

```text
plan complete
implementation complete
tests complete
review complete
```

This improves recovery and operator visibility.

Do not confuse checkpoints with Anthropic session internals.

---

# 81. CODING WORKFLOW TEMPLATE

Ship at least one robust workflow:

```text
analyze
→ plan
→ implement
→ test
→ review
→ fix
→ final validate
```

But allow shorter workflows.

---

# 82. REVIEW AGENT

Optional independent reviewer should inspect actual diff.

Review categories:

```text
correctness
security
tests
backward compatibility
architecture
performance
```

It must not automatically rewrite implementation unless workflow permits it.

---

# 83. TEST-FIX LOOP

Support bounded repair loop:

```text
implementation
→ tests fail
→ diagnose
→ repair
→ rerun
```

Maximum attempts must be configurable.

Avoid infinite autonomous loops.

---

# 84. STOP CONDITIONS

Each job needs explicit stop conditions:

```text
max attempts
budget
wall-clock
operator cancellation
no progress
validation success
fatal policy violation
```

---

# 85. NO-PROGRESS DETECTION

Detect simple pathological loops.

Signals may include:

```text
same failure repeated
same tool invocation repeatedly
same patch repeatedly
no file changes
```

Do not rely on semantic similarity alone for correctness.

---

# 86. CONTEXT MANAGEMENT

Reuse backend-native context management where suitable.

Do not independently summarize context if Agent SDK/Managed Agents already provides appropriate compaction unless ZCoder has a specific reason.

Track context-management events for diagnostics.

---

# 87. BACKEND PORTABILITY TEST

Create a small test workflow runnable across:

```text
DirectRuntime
MockAgentSDKRuntime
MockManagedRuntime
```

Validate that core ZCoder job lifecycle remains backend-independent.

---

# 88. FAKE RUNTIME

Create a deterministic FakeRuntime for tests.

Capabilities:

```text
scripted events
forced failure
budget reached
approval requested
retry
cancel
resume
```

Most orchestration tests should not require Anthropic credentials.

---

# 89. CONTRACT TESTS

For each runtime adapter test:

```text
start
events
status
cancel
resume
error mapping
```

For Managed Agents also verify actual documented wire shapes independently.

---

# 90. STATE-MACHINE TESTS

Test every legal and illegal state transition.

Property-based testing is welcome if useful.

Examples:

```text
SUCCEEDED -> RUNNING     invalid
CANCELLED -> resume      policy-dependent
WAITING_APPROVAL -> RUNNING after approval
BUDGET_REACHED -> resume after valid budget change
```

---

# 91. CRASH TESTS

Persist job.

Destroy orchestrator object.

Recreate.

Resume/reconcile.

Prove state survives.

---

# 92. CONCURRENCY TESTS

Test:

```text
two jobs
same repo
different worktrees

two jobs
same idempotency key

cancel during tool execution

approval arriving during reconciliation
```

Avoid timing-fragile sleeps.

---

# 93. SECURITY TESTS

Add tests proving:

* secrets are never event payloads
* denied tool cannot execute
* workspace traversal blocked
* unsafe Git mutation requires approval
* cancellation terminates child processes
* malformed webhook rejected
* policy invalid -> fail closed
* untrusted memory cannot silently gain trusted status

---

# 94. COST TESTS

Test:

```text
normal completion
budget warning
budget reached
resume with larger budget
multiagent usage aggregation
provider cost + runtime cost
```

Expected values should be independently expressed.

---

# 95. INTEGRATION TEST

Build one end-to-end offline scenario:

```text
create temporary Git repo

insert failing Python test

submit ZCoder job using FakeRuntime

agent produces patch

validator runs pytest

job succeeds

inspect:
  events
  artifact
  diff
  cost
  final state
```

No Anthropic key required.

---

# 96. OPTIONAL LIVE TESTS

Create separately marked tests for:

```text
Agent SDK
Managed Agents
```

Example:

```bash
pytest -m anthropic_live_agent_sdk

pytest -m anthropic_live_managed
```

They must be disabled by default.

Keep live tests minimal-cost.

Never send private repository content from CI unexpectedly.

---

# 97. RELEASE GATE INTEGRATION

Extend Upgrade-05 release gate.

Add:

```text
AGENT_RUNTIME
JOB_STATE_MACHINE
PERSISTENCE
APPROVAL_POLICY
SANDBOX
ARTIFACTS
RUNTIME_ADAPTERS
```

Example:

```text
SOURCE_TRUTH: PASS
TESTS: PASS
BUILD: PASS
INSTALL: PASS
AGENT_RUNTIME: PASS
JOB_STATE_MACHINE: PASS
PERSISTENCE: PASS
APPROVAL_POLICY: PASS
SECURITY: PASS
```

---

# 98. CLI TARGET

By the end of Upgrade-06, aim for a coherent experience approximately like:

```bash
zcoder run "Fix failing tests"

zcoder run "Review authentication" \
  --runtime agent-sdk

zcoder run "Migrate API and run all tests" \
  --runtime managed \
  --budget-usd 10

zcoder jobs list

zcoder jobs show job_123

zcoder jobs watch job_123

zcoder jobs threads job_123

zcoder jobs artifacts job_123

zcoder jobs cancel job_123

zcoder jobs retry job_123

zcoder jobs resume job_123

zcoder approvals list

zcoder approvals approve apr_123
```

Exact commands may differ if existing CLI architecture suggests a better design.

Keep old flags working.

---

# 99. DO NOT DO THESE THINGS

Do NOT:

* rewrite the whole repository
* create duplicate Anthropic clients
* implement fake provider APIs
* claim resume where impossible
* hide tests
* create unbounded autonomous loops
* grant shell/network access globally
* put secrets in the event database
* automatically force-push Git
* automatically publish packages
* automatically deploy production changes
* automatically approve destructive operations
* use Managed Agents as if it were the Agent SDK
* use Agent SDK as if it were Managed Agents
* require network access for normal unit tests
* make preview features production dependencies
* add Postgres/Redis/Kafka/Kubernetes without a demonstrated requirement

---

# 100. VERSION DECISION

Upgrade-06 introduces significant backward-compatible functionality.

If v1.40.0 remains unreleased and Upgrade-06 materially changes scope, decide whether this work should become:

```text
v1.41.0
```

or a later minor release.

Do not casually keep stacking major new runtime architecture into an already-defined release candidate.

Explain the SemVer decision.

---

# 101. REQUIRED DOCUMENTATION

Update:

```text
README.md
CHANGELOG.md
ROADMAP.md
ARCHITECTURE.md
SECURITY.md
CONTRIBUTING.md
```

Add or update:

```text
docs/UPGRADE-06.md
docs/AGENT-RUNTIME.md
docs/JOB-LIFECYCLE.md
docs/RUNTIME-ADAPTERS.md
docs/TOOL-POLICY.md
docs/APPROVALS.md
docs/SANDBOX.md
docs/AGENT-MEMORY.md
```

Do not create empty placeholder documentation.

---

# 102. REQUIRED FINAL VERIFICATION

Run:

```bash
python -m pytest -q
python main.py --help
python main.py --version
python -m build
git diff --check
git status --short
```

Run full optional-dependency suite.

Run package clean-install smoke test.

Run the release gate.

If available:

```bash
ruff check .
black --check .
mypy .
```

Do not omit failed commands from the final report.

---

# 103. DEFINITION OF DONE

Upgrade-06 is complete only when:

* [ ] durable ZCoder Job model exists
* [ ] job state survives process restart
* [ ] structured event history exists
* [ ] runtime adapter interface exists
* [ ] Direct runtime is preserved
* [ ] Agent SDK runtime is integrated
* [ ] Managed Agents runtime is integrated
* [ ] runtime capabilities are explicit
* [ ] local and remote session IDs are persisted correctly
* [ ] operator approval engine exists
* [ ] tool policy engine exists
* [ ] dangerous mutations are gated
* [ ] workspace safety exists
* [ ] concurrent jobs cannot corrupt the same worktree
* [ ] cancellation works
* [ ] retries are bounded
* [ ] resume/reconciliation works where supported
* [ ] artifacts and diffs are recorded
* [ ] validation pipeline determines job success
* [ ] cost/budget accounting exists
* [ ] secrets do not enter event storage
* [ ] FakeRuntime enables offline tests
* [ ] runtime adapters have contract tests
* [ ] state machine has exhaustive tests
* [ ] at least one end-to-end offline coding workflow passes
* [ ] CLI exposes coherent job/operator commands
* [ ] TUI/Web use shared job services where implemented
* [ ] release gate includes agent-runtime correctness
* [ ] normal CI requires no Anthropic credential
* [ ] docs match actual implementation
* [ ] no P0/P1 runtime/security defects remain

---

# 104. FINAL REPORT FORMAT

Return exactly:

## 1. Baseline

Repository/version/test state before Upgrade-06.

## 2. Architecture

Old architecture versus new runtime architecture.

## 3. Job Runtime

Job model, lifecycle and persistence.

## 4. Runtime Adapters

Table:

Runtime | Backend | Durable | Resume | Multiagent | Sandbox | Memory | Status

## 5. Agent Orchestration

Planner, DAG, multiagent and validation workflow.

## 6. Workspace & Git Safety

Worktrees, dirty-tree policy and change tracking.

## 7. Approval & Tool Policy

Security behavior and operator UX.

## 8. Managed Agents Integration

Agents, environments, sessions, threads, events, budgets and supported optional capabilities.

## 9. Agent SDK Integration

Sessions, subagents, hooks, tools, skills, MCP and permissions.

## 10. Persistence & Recovery

Crash recovery, reconciliation and idempotency.

## 11. Cost & Budget

Provider usage, runtime cost and budget enforcement.

## 12. Memory / Skills / MCP

Architecture and security boundaries.

## 13. CLI

Exact commands added or changed.

## 14. Web / TUI

Shared-service integration status.

## 15. Tests

Exact new tests and final totals.

## 16. Security Review

Threats found and mitigations.

## 17. Release Gate

Report:

SOURCE_TRUTH:
TESTS:
BUILD:
INSTALL:
JOB_RUNTIME:
RUNTIME_ADAPTERS:
PERSISTENCE:
APPROVAL_POLICY:
SANDBOX:
SECURITY:
DOCS:

FINAL:

## 18. Version Decision

Chosen release version and SemVer reasoning.

## 19. Files Changed

Exact paths.

## 20. Remaining Gaps

Only genuine unresolved limitations.

## 21. Final Verdict

Do not claim:

PRODUCTION READY

unless all mandatory gates are PASS.

Do not call a feature implemented merely because a stub, CLI flag, documentation page or mock exists.

Implementation evidence must include working code plus tests.
